  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>(A) The Fermi-level in an intrinsic semiconductor is nearly midway between the conductive and valence band.So,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi>A</mi></mfenced><mo>&#8594;</mo><mi>I</mi><mi>I</mi></math>.</p><p>(B)&nbsp;In the case of n-type semiconductor, the Fermi level is present just below the conduction band. So,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi>B</mi></mfenced><mo>&#8594;</mo><mi>I</mi><mi>I</mi><mi>I</mi></math>.</p><p>(C)&nbsp;Fermi level in a p-type semiconductor lies close to the top of the valence band. So,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi>C</mi></mfenced><mo>&#8594;</mo><mi>I</mi></math></p><p>(D)&nbsp;In metals and semimetals the Fermi level&nbsp;lies inside at least one band i.e., inside conduction band. So,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi>D</mi></mfenced><mo>&#8594;</mo><mi>I</mi><mi>V</mi></math>.</p>"
